<subsection xml:id="subsection-1-4-2">
    <title>Derivative Shortcuts</title>
    <p>
        Fortunately, there are algebraic shortcuts for finding the derivative function of any elementary function.  
        We give a list of those rules below.  
        First, recall the notation <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} f(x)</m>.  
        This is an operator meaning "the derivative of <m>f(x)</m>.""
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-derivative-shortcuts">
        <title>Derivative Shortcut Rules</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For any constant <m>c</m>, <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} c = 0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        For any constant <m>c</m>, <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} cx = c</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        For any real number <m>n</m>, <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} x^n = nx^{n-1}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        For any <m>a>0</m>, <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} a^x = a^x \ln(a)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} e^x = e^x</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \log_a(x) = \frac{1}{\ln(a) x}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \ln(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \sin(x) = \cos(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \cos(x) = -\sin(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \arctan(x) = \frac{1}{1+x^2}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Constant Rule: <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} c\cdot f(x) = c\cdot f'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sum Rule: <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} [f(x) \pm g(x)] = f'(x) \pm g'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chain Rule: <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))\cdot g'(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Product Rule: <m>\displaystyle {\frac{d}{dx} \left(f(x)\cdot g(x)\right) = f(x)\cdot g'(x) + f'(x) \cdot g(x)}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Quotient Rule: <m>\displaystyle {\frac{d}{dx} \left(\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right) = \frac{g(x)\cdot f'(x) - f(x)\cdot g'(x)}{[g(x)]^2}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        "Mini Chain Rule": <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} f(ax+b) = a\cdot f'(ax+b)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
        Pay attention to the last rule, that I like to call the "mini chain rule."
        Many students pick this up after doing a number of derivative examples, but I find it helpful to see it explicitly.
        If you have a function that is a composition of functions, but the inside function is linear, then the derivative is simply
        the derivative of the outside function, with the inside function plugged in, times the slope of the linear function.
        For example, the derivative of <m>{\sin(-2x+7)}</m> is <m>\cos(-2x+7) \cdot (-2) = -2\cos(-2x+7)}</m>.
    </p>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-derivative-shortcuts">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the derivative functions of the following functions.
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle 5x^{4.3} - e^{7x} + 3</m>
                        </p>
                   </li>

                   <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle (9-x^2)^8 + \frac{5}{x^3}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle 9\arctan({5x^2})</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \ln(7x)\cos(x)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle 5^{x}-\frac{8x^2-1}{\ln(5x-2)}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Applying the chain rule to <m>\displaystyle e^{7x}</m>, we get <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} (5x^{4.3} - e^{7x} + 3) = 5\cdot 4.3 x^{3.3} - e^{7x}\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} 7x\right) + 0 = 21.5x^{3.3} - 7e^{7x}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Applying the chain rule to <m>(9-x^2)^8</m> and noticing that <m>\frac{5}{x^3} = 5x^{-3}</m>, we get 
                            <m>{\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \left( (9-x^2)^8 + \frac{5}{x^3}\right) = 8(9-x^2)^7 \cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} 9-x^2\right) + 5\cdot (-3)x^{-4} = -16x(9-x^2)^7 - \frac{15}{x^4}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Applying the chain rule to <m>\arctan(5x^2)</m>, we get
                            <m>{\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \left(9\arctan(5x^2)\right) = 9\cdot \frac{1}{1+(5x^2)^2}\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} 5x^2\right) = \frac{9}{1+25x^4}\cdot 10x = \frac{90x}{1+25x^4}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Applying the chain rule to <m>\ln(7x)</m> yields its derivative as <m>{\displaystyle \frac{7}{7x} = \frac{1}{x}}</m>.  
                            Then, we use the product rule to get
                            <m>{\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \left(\ln(7x)\cos(x)\right) = \ln(7x) \cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} \cos(x)\right) + \left(\frac{d}{dx} \ln(7x)\right)\cdot \cos(x)} {= -\ln(7x)\sin(x) + \frac{\cos(x)}{x}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We will need to use the chain rule for <m>\ln(5x-2)</m> to get its derivative of <m>\displaystyle \frac{5}{5x-2}</m>.  
                            Then applying the quotient rule yields
                            <m>{\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx} \left(5^x - \frac{8x^2 - 1}{\ln(5x-2)}\right) = \ln(5) \cdot 5^x - \frac{\ln(5x-2)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} 8x^2 - 1\right) - (8x^2-1)\cdot \left(\frac{d}{dx} \ln(5x-2)\right)}{[\ln(5x-2)]^2}} {= \ln(5)\cdot 5^x - \frac{\ln(5x-2)\cdot (16x) - (8x^2 - 1)\cdot \frac{5}{5x-2}}{[\ln(5x-2)]^2}}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
        Fortunately, you will not need to calculate derivatives that are as complicated as the preceding ones on the P exam.
    </p>
</subsection>